---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import MigrationNameGenerator from '../../components/MigrationNameGenerator.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="Database Migration Naming Generator" description="Generate a properly formatted migration filename from a description — timestamp, sequential, or Unix style.">
  <MigrationNameGenerator client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why migration naming conventions matter</h2>
  <p>Migration tools order and track migrations by their filename — an inconsistent or incorrectly formatted name can cause migrations to run out of order or be silently skipped, which is especially dangerous for schema changes that depend on previous ones.</p>

  <h2>How to use this generator</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Enter a short description of the migration.</li>
    <li>Choose the naming style matching your framework (timestamp, sequential, Unix timestamp).</li>
    <li>Copy the generated filename.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Example</h2>
  <p>"add email verified column to users" with timestamp style generates something like <code>20250115093000_add_email_verified_column_to_users.sql</code>.</p>

  <h2>Common Migration Naming Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Mixing naming styles within the same project</strong>, confusing the migration tool's ordering logic.</li>
    <li><strong>Reusing a timestamp or number</strong> from an existing migration, causing a naming collision.</li>
    <li><strong>Writing vague descriptions</strong> like "fix" or "update" that don't explain what the migration actually does months later.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to Use This Migration Name Generator</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Creating a new migration file following your framework's expected naming convention</li>
    <li>Standardizing migration naming across a team</li>
    <li>Quickly generating a properly formatted name without checking documentation each time</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Migration Naming FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Which naming style should I use?</strong> Match whatever your specific migration tool/framework expects — Rails and Django use timestamp-based naming, some tools use sequential numbers, and Node-based tools like Knex often use Unix timestamps.</p>
</ToolLayout>
